$$
\begin{align}
& x_1,\,x_2 \\
\begin{cases}
x_1+x_2=-a \\
((x_1+x_2)^2-2x_1x_2)=11 \\
x_1x_2=\frac{6}{x_1}
\end{cases}
\end{align}
$$
$$
\begin{align}
& x_3,\,x_4 \\
\begin{cases}
x_3+x_4=-b \\
((x_3+x_4)^2-2x_3x_4)=14 \\
x_3x_4=\frac{8}{x_3}
\end{cases}
\end{align}
$$
Adding:
$$
\begin{align}
x_1,x_2;
\quad x_3,x_4
\quad ; \\
x_1+x_2=-a;
\quad x_3+x_4=-b \\
\end{align}
$$

After multiplying each other:
$$
\begin{align}
x_1;\,x_2 \quad x_3\,,x_4 \quad \text{are distinct roots}\\
x_1+x_2+x_3+x_4=-a-b \\
\end{align}
$$
Cross multiplying:
$$
\begin{align}
x_1*x_2=x_3*x_4 \\
x^2-11x+6=0;\\
x^2-14x+8=0; \\
\end{align}
$$ and so:
$$\left(\frac{11}2\right)^2-\frac{1}{11} = \left(\frac{14}2\right)^2-14, \quad \frac{m \in \mathbb{Z}}{x^2+mx+n}\in \mathbb{Z}$$

Solving the previous quadratic equation:
\begin{align*}
x^2-11x+6&=0\\
(x-2)(x-3)&=0 \\
x&=2 \lor x=3
\end{align*}
and:
\begin{align*}
x^2-14x+8&=0\\
(x-1)(x-8)&=0 \\
x&=1 \lor x=8
\end{align*}

Combining the roots, we get:
$$\{(2,3), (1,8), (1,3), (2,8)\}$$

Therefore, the ordered pair $(a,b)$ is $(6,9)$.

